Feature Plan: BookController split-out migration (controllers/services/records) + BookingHandlerImpl removal

Objective
- Split the current `BookController` into `MovieController`, `VenueController`,
  `PurchaseController`, and `CheckoutController`, rolling the record/mapper pattern out to each
  endpoint family with zero JSON contract change.
- Move the DTO/domain-shaping work out of `BookingHandlerImpl` and into dedicated services
  (`MovieService`, `VenueService`, `PurchaseService`, `CheckoutService`) so the old handler can be
  removed once the migration is complete.
- Relocate `fullcheckout2`'s inline reservation/payment orchestration into `CheckoutService`.
- Bind multi-input controller methods via `@BeanParam` classes instead of individual annotated
  parameters.
- Introduce `ApiResponseFactory`, a shared CDI bean for building success/error `Response`s, with
  the status code passed in by the caller rather than hard-coded per call site.

Role ownership
- Primary: `@backend-dev`
- Contract/parity verification: `@ios-dev`, `@frontend-dev`
- Payment-path review: `@backend-dev` (Braintree integration risk)

Baseline (as-found)
- `BookController.java` (1079 lines) mixes request parsing, validation, business orchestration,
  and manual JSON assembly (`JSONObject`/`JSONArray`/`HashMap<String,String>`) in nearly every
  method.
- `BookingHandlerImpl.java` is a broad DAO façade plus DTO/domain-shaping layer and is currently
  injected directly by `BookController` and the existing helper services.
- `PurchaseService.java` currently only has `getBrainTreeCustomer(String uuid)`; it does not yet
  own any checkout orchestration.
- `TicketService`/`PaymentService` already exist and are called directly from `BookController` in
  `fullcheckout2`.
- Trending-movies migration (separate spec/plan) establishes the pattern: top-level records in
  `com.jeet.api`, one `@ApplicationScoped` mapper per family, controller keeps its `Response`
  return type and validation/error branches untouched.
- No `ApiResponseFactory` or shared `@BeanParam` param classes exist yet; every controller method
  builds its own `Response` inline and reads params individually.

Phased implementation sequence
- use modell for all like in speckit.backendendpoints.movies.implementation-example.md
(each phase = one family from the spec's grouping; implement all phases in one pass, no
intermediate compile/verification between phases — see Verification section below and
REQ-ALL-6/NF-2)

Phase 0 — Shared building blocks
- Add `com.jeet.rest.ApiResponseFactory` (`@ApplicationScoped`): a success builder overloaded as
  `ok(Object body)` (defaults to `200`) and `ok(Object body, Response.Status status)` (for
  non-`200` success cases, e.g. `201`), plus an error builder that takes a status
  code/`Response.Status` plus an error body, both setting `MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON`.
- No controller wiring yet in this phase — just introduce and unit-test the factory in isolation
  so Phases 1-4 can inject it directly.
- Do not touch existing `BookController` error shapes yet; the factory is additive infrastructure.

Phase 1 — Movie list family
- Create `MovieController` and `MovieService`.
- Move movie-list/search/trending-related endpoint logic out of `BookController` and into
  `MovieService`.
- Add `MovieSummaryItem` record + one mapper (`MovieMapper` or similar) with methods covering
  `getAllMovies`, `getAllMoviesWithPaging`, `searchMovies`, `fullTextSearchMovies`,
  `getMoviesForVenue`, and trending-movies response assembly.
- Add `@BeanParam` classes for multi-param endpoints in this family (e.g. `TrendingMoviesParams`
  for `days`/`limit`, a paging-params class for `getAllMoviesWithPaging`); leave
  single-param endpoints (e.g. `getMoviesForVenue`'s single `locationId`) on plain
  `@QueryParam`/`@PathParam`.
- Wire `MovieController` to use `ApiResponseFactory` for both success and validation-error
  responses instead of inline `Response.status(...).entity(...).build()`.
- Verify each endpoint's root key (`movies` vs `searchedMovies` vs `trendingMovies`) is preserved
  per-endpoint.

Phase 2 — Venue/location + screen admin family
- Create `VenueController` and `VenueService`.
- Move venue/location/date/seat endpoint logic out of `BookController` and into `VenueService`.
- Add `VenueScreeningItem` record + one mapper reused by `getAllMoviesOnAllVenues`,
  `getAllMoviesOnAllVenuesWithCategory`, `getAllMoviesOnAllVenuesForSearch` (identical per-item
  shape today — single mapper method, three call sites).
- Confirm which venue/location/date/seat endpoints still return entities directly and only
  introduce records where manual JSON assembly exists today.
- Also move `addScreen`, `updateScreen`, `deleteScreen` into `VenueController`/`VenueService`
  (screens/screenings are venue-scoped). Introduce response record(s) for their success/`409
  CONFLICT` bodies; leave `addScreen`'s async Kafka publish call and its serialization untouched.
- Reuse `ApiResponseFactory` (from Phase 0) for all `VenueController` responses; add `@BeanParam`
  classes only for endpoints with more than one bound input (e.g. `getDatesForMovieOnVenue`'s
  `locationId`+`movieId` path params).

Phase 3 — Purchases/tickets family
- Create `PurchaseController` and `PurchaseService`.
- Naming/ownership decision (resolve before or at the start of this phase): existing
  `com.jeet.booking.PurchaseService` only has `getBrainTreeCustomer(String uuid)`, which
  `CheckoutService` (Phase 4) also depends on. Either extend that existing class in place with
  this family's new methods, or introduce a differently-named/-scoped service for this family —
  do not create a second, ambiguous `PurchaseService` under the same name.
- Move purchase/ticket/cancel/delete endpoint logic out of `BookController` and into
  `PurchaseService`.
- Add `PurchaseSummaryItem`, `TicketSummaryItem` records + mapper(s) for `getAllPurchases`,
  `getTicketsPerPurchase`.
- Reuse `ApiResponseFactory`; add a `@BeanParam` class for `cancelTicket`'s combined
  header/body-derived inputs if more than one bound input remains after moving parsing logic into
  the service.

Phase 4 — Checkout family
- Create `CheckoutController` and `CheckoutService`.
- Move `fullcheckout2` orchestration out of `BookController` and into `CheckoutService`.
- `clientToken` migrates into `CheckoutController` alongside `fullcheckout2`.
  (`webcheckout` and `getMovieTicket` have since been removed from `BookController` and are no
  longer part of this phase.)
- Add response record(s) for `fullcheckout2`'s success path (fields: `ResponseText`, `purchaseId`,
  `AuthCode`, `ResponseCode`, `Status`, `Amount`, `TaxAmount`, `Success`, `tickets`,
  `seatsforscreen`).
- Add a `FullCheckoutParams` `@BeanParam` class (header `uuid` + form `orderId`/
  `seatsToBeReserved`/`payment_method_nonce`) replacing today's manual
  `HttpServletRequest`/`HttpHeaders` parameter reads; keep parsing/validation helper methods on
  the class itself, business logic stays in `CheckoutService`.
- Reuse `ApiResponseFactory` for `fullcheckout2`'s success/error responses (error JSON shape
  unchanged — see spec's Out of scope note on error-shape unification).

Phase 5 — Service extraction from BookingHandlerImpl
- Move the DTO/domain-shaping methods out of `BookingHandlerImpl` into the four dedicated
  services.
- Keep the DAO calls and any genuinely shared persistence helpers in the lower layer.
- Remove `BookingHandlerImpl` once the new services fully replace all controller call sites.

Phase 6 — Trivial/leftover
- `getGreeting` migrated opportunistically if convenient; not a dedicated phase.

Verification (bulk, at the end — no intermediate compile per REQ-ALL-6/NF-2)
1. Complete all phases (0-6) in one pass without stopping to compile in between.
2. `cd mbooks-quarkus && mvn -DskipTests compile` once, after all phases are done.
3. Manual curl (or existing test) comparing before/after JSON for every migrated endpoint in one
   consolidated pass.
4. Run any existing endpoint tests covering the touched methods.
5. Confirm existing status codes and error JSON shapes are unchanged after routing through
   `ApiResponseFactory` (the factory takes status as a parameter — a wrong call-site argument is
   the main regression risk).
6. Changes can be safely reverted from the pre-migration git commit if the bulk verification
   surfaces regressions.

Risks
- `fullcheckout2` orchestration is the highest-risk phase (payment side effects); land it after the
  lower-risk read-endpoint families have validated the record/mapper pattern at scale.
- Endpoints returning entities directly (not manually built JSON) should not be forced into new
  records just for consistency — only migrate where manual JSON assembly exists today.
- Root JSON key naming is inconsistent across endpoints (`movies` vs `searchedMovies`, singular
  vs plural) — preserve as-is per endpoint; do not unify naming as part of this migration.
- `ApiResponseFactory`'s error builder taking a status code as a parameter makes it easy to
  accidentally pass the wrong status at a call site during migration; the single bulk
  verification pass at the end must check status codes, not just body shape.
- Existing error-body shapes differ across endpoints/exceptions (`CustomExceptions`'s
  `Title`/`Message` vs trending-movies' `error`/`message`); the factory must not silently
  normalize these during the migration — each call site keeps its current shape.

Definition of done
- All families in the spec are migrated or explicitly deferred with a documented reason.
- `fullcheckout2` no longer contains inline seat-reservation/payment-orchestration code; that
  logic lives in `CheckoutService`.
- `BookingHandlerImpl` is removed after its call sites are replaced by the dedicated services.
- `ApiResponseFactory` exists and is used by all four new controllers; no controller builds a raw
  `Response` inline except where a body/status combination the factory doesn't yet support is
  encountered (should be rare given the factory takes status as a parameter).
- Multi-input controller methods use `@BeanParam` classes; single-input methods are left as-is.
- No JSON contract regressions found during the single bulk verification pass at the end.
- Module compiles once, at the end of all phases (no intermediate per-phase compiles, per
  REQ-ALL-6/NF-2).
